`getManifest(JarFile, JarEntry)` now propagates `IOException` up to `forUrl()`, which can cause the entire jar to be skipped if the embedded jar is missing/corrupt or the manifest can't be read. Previously, the embedded-jar manifest read swallowed `IOException` and returned `null` (matching the non-embedded `getManifest(JarFile)` behavior). Consider catching `IOException` here (or at the call site) and returning `null` to preserve the previous behavior and avoid failing metadata extraction due to a best-effort field.
